  "raw": "{\n  \"audits\": [\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q001\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\n        \"math:modular-congruence\",\n        \"math:divisor-count-and-sum\"\n      ],\n      \"supportIds\": [\n        \"math:prime-factorization\"\n      ],\n      \"routes\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"ordered-full-remainder-list\",\n          \"label\": \"Order the full remainder list\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\n            \"math:modular-congruence\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"positive-residue-increments\",\n          \"label\": \"Positive residue increments\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\n            \"math:modular-congruence\",\n            \"cnm_097\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"E: 4319\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Write r_k for the remainder of 2025k modulo z. The condition requires 0 = r_0 < r_1 < ... < r_{z-1} ≤ z-1. These z distinct integers must therefore be exactly 0,1,...,z-1, so 2025 ≡ 1 (mod z), equivalently z divides 2024. Conversely, that congruence gives r_k = k for every 0 ≤ k < z, proving sufficiency. Since 2024 = 2^3·11·23, the requested sum is (1+2+4+8)(1+11)(1+23)−1 = 4319; subtracting 1 excludes z = 1. The alternative increment route is also valid: with r = r_1 > 0, each successive difference is either r or r−z. Positivity excludes the latter, giving (z−1)r ≤ z−1. Because z−1 > 0, this linear inequality yields r ≤ 1 and hence r = 1. Thus cnm_097 is defensible within that optional route, without being universally required. The assessed and supporting scopes fit the congruence characterization and divisor-sum calculation.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"questionId\": \"q002\",\n      \"decision\": \"accept\",\n      \"assessedIds\": [\n        \"cnm_047\"\n      ],\n      \"supportIds\": [],\n      \"routes\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"total-distance-ratio\",\n          \"label\": \"Compare total distances\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\n            \"cnm_p213\",\n            \"cnm_042\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"outbound-and-return\",\n          \"label\": \"Separate the two travel stages\",\n          \"nodeIds\": [\n            \"cnm_047\",\n            \"cnm_p111\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"derivedAnswer\": \"D: 1/3\",\n      \"mathematicalReason\": \"Let L > 0 be the entrance-to-tree distance and d Miguel's distance when Luna returns. Over the same elapsed time, Luna travels five times Miguel's distance. Her outbound and return distances total L+(L−d), so 5d = 2L−d, giving d/L = 1/3. This is consistent with meeting before Miguel reaches the tree. The total-distance route validly uses direct proportion and a linear equation. Alternatively, if Miguel's speed is v > 0, the outbound time is L/(5v), leaving a separation of 4L/5. The return closing speed is 6v, so the return time is 2L/(15v). Miguel consequently travels L/5+2L/15 = L/3. Both routes are defensible, and travel/meeting modeling is an appropriate assessed scope; neither optional method needs to be treated as universally required.\",\n      \"candidateFindings\": [],\n      \"needsNewNodes\": [],\n      \"limitations\": [],\n      \"figureEvidence\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}",
